  "narrative": "During the 1980s, Black and African women's hairstyles and hair culture were thriving with a rich tapestry of traditions and innovations. The natural hair movement, which had originated in the 1960s, continued to gain momentum, encouraging individuals to celebrate their afro-textured hair. Box braids, a protective style that had become increasingly popular among Black Africans and the African diaspora, were a staple in many communities. Meanwhile, the Jheri curl, a chemical treatment that gave wearers a glossy, loosely curled look, was all the rage among Black Americans. The afro, a hairstyle created by combing out natural growth or styled with chemical products, remained a symbol of black identity and pride. While documentation from this era is limited, it's clear that hairstyles like cornrows, dreadlocks (also known as locs), and Bantu knots were also part of the cultural landscape, reflecting the diversity and creativity of Black and African hair culture during the 1980s.",
